{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "avg-10-average-speed-equal-distances",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Average Speed for Equal Distances",
    "description": "Understand why equal-distance journeys use a harmonic-style average, derive 2xy/(x+y), and know when not to use it.",
    "teachingApproach": "Begin by disproving the tempting arithmetic mean with actual travel times. Derive 2xy/(x+y) from total distance ÷ total time, practise direct substitution, reverse the formula to find a missing speed, use speed ratios and a sanity check, then contrast unequal-distance journeys before a round-trip capstone that verifies the shortcut with time.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "Teach the chapter's equal-distance formula Average speed = 2xy/(x+y) by first comparing actual travel times on a short out-and-back journey. Show why the simple arithmetic mean is wrong because the slower leg lasts longer. Derive the formula from total distance ÷ total time, practise direct substitution and solving for a missing return speed, use speed ratios, and build a sanity check that the result lies between the two speeds and below their arithmetic mean when the speeds differ. Explicitly contrast equal-distance and unequal-distance journeys; unequal distances must return to total distance ÷ total time."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's finish Average with the one formula that looks unusual until you see the time behind it.\n\nFor equal outward and return distances, the slower leg lasts longer — so the true average speed is not usually the simple mean of the two speeds.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Imagine a 100 km trip out at 60 km/h and the same 100 km back at 40 km/h. It is tempting to average 60 and 40 and say 50.\n\nBut the slower leg lasts longer.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Time out = 100/60 = 5/3 h\nTime back = 100/40 = 5/2 h\nTotal time = 25/6 h\n\nAverage speed = 200 ÷ (25/6) = 48 km/h\n\nThe true average is pulled toward the slower speed.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "A vehicle covers equal distances at 80 km/h and 40 km/h. What is its average speed?",
